cairn NEWS — history of user-visible changes.

Copyright (C) 2026 Oddur Sigurdsson.
See the end of the file for copying conditions.

Please send bug reports and suggestions through the project's issue tracker.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0-rc.2 (unreleased)

** The promise

PROMISE.md states what cairn will always do and where its boundary falls: every
capability a single repository has is part of cairn and is free, and cairn will
never grow accounts, authentication or remotes.  The same words appear in the
README, the manual and the website, and `cargo test --test rules` fails if any
copy drifts from another.

It is written now, while cairn has no users and nothing to sell, because that
is the only moment at which making the promise costs nothing.

** Integrating

A new manual chapter, "Integrating", documents the two supported ways to build
on cairn: read the specified file format, or run the binary and parse its JSON.
There is deliberately no library target, because linking cairn makes the caller
a derivative work and reading its files does not.  A test fails if a [lib]
appears in Cargo.toml.

** A cookbook, and a test that the manual is true

A new Cookbook chapter, and a page on the site, with recipes for what people
arrive wanting to do: adopting cairn where GitHub issues exist, weekly triage,
wiring it into CI, scoping a slice of the backlog for an agent, moving a backlog
between schemas, finding stalled work, and recovering after a bad merge.

tests/manual.rs extracts every command the manual shows and runs it against the
real program, failing on any the program would reject.  Documentation was the
least-tested surface here, and had twice claimed a flag nobody implemented.  It
found two problems on its first run, one of which was a genuine gap:

`cairn claim --next` now takes --filter, so an agent can claim from a scoped
slice.  Scoping the claim rather than the listing is what makes it safe — doing
it as list-then-claim leaves a gap in which another claimer can take the item,
which is the gap the lock exists to close.

** Translation

cairn is not translated and will not be before 1.0, and the manual now says so
as a position rather than leaving it an oversight.  Most of what cairn prints is
prose rather than labels, a half-translated diagnostic cannot be searched for,
and the cost is sustained maintenance rather than one-off extraction.  What
would reverse it is written down: a contributor volunteering to own a language.

** Merges keep the published identifier

When two branches each allocated the same identifier, `cairn renumber` moved the
wrong one about half the time: both items looked identical to it — same creation
date, distinguished only by filename — so it picked alphabetically.  Renaming
the side that was already published churns history and breaks every link written
to it.

cairn now asks the repository.  If the current commit is a merge, its first
parent is the branch the merge was made on, and the item added there keeps its
number.  Outside a repository the previous rule applies unchanged.

** A second implementation of the reader

spec/reader.py is a short reader for the item format, written from the
specification alone and not from cairn's source, and permissively licensed so it
can be copied into other tools including proprietary ones.  spec/conformance.py
runs it over the corpus in tests/golden and compares its answers to cairn's; CI
runs it on every push.

It found a defect in the specification immediately.  §6 said unquoted scalars
follow "YAML's own rules" without saying which YAML — and 1.1 and 1.2 disagree
about `no` and `12:30`, two of the three examples the section itself gave.  Two
conforming readers could have reported different content for the same file.  §6
now requires the YAML 1.2 core schema and tabulates the divergence.

** Identifiers can be written the way your team says them

  [project]
  id_format = "MP-{n}"    # MP-1002, A24, CAIRN-TOOLS-24
  id_start  = 1000        # where an empty project starts counting

`{n}` is the number and `{n:04}` pads it; the default is unchanged.  Files are
named to match, and both the rendered form and the bare number are accepted
wherever an identifier is taken, case-insensitively.

The key is a rendering rather than the identifier.  `id` in the frontmatter
remains an unsigned integer, which is what the specification commits to, so
adopting a key is a display change and not a format change — no version bump, no
migration, and nothing that refers to an item by number breaks.  In JSON, `id`
is the number and `ref` is the rendered form.

`cairn renumber` now also brings filenames into line with the configured format,
which is what `cairn check` has been reporting.  A malformed template is refused
when the project is opened, naming the problem.

Specification §4.2 records all of this: it is a clarification, the format stays
at 1, and nothing already written changes meaning.

** What may be scripted against

A new "Stability" chapter in the manual says which surfaces are promises and
which are deliberately free to change.  Promised: the JSON fields, --ids,
--plain and --count, the filter grammar, the MCP tool names and schemas, the
interchange document, and the exit codes (0 did it, 1 failed or found problems,
2 the command line was wrong).  Not promised: the wording of anything written
for a person, colour, table layout, and the order of anything not documented as
ordered.

tests/stability.rs asserts every documented field, so removing one fails the
build rather than somebody's script.

** The command surface, reviewed before 1.0

`cairn renumber --compact` has been removed.  It renumbered a whole project into
a gapless sequence, moving every identifier — which silently invalidates every
commit message, pull request and human memory that referred to one, and cairn
cannot rewrite any of those.  A large irreversible cost for a cosmetic benefit,
and nothing outside its own test ever wanted it.  Gaps are now permanent by
design.

`cairn next` and `cairn search` gained --plain and --count, which they lacked
for no reason anybody could name.  Output shapes belong on every listing
command; query controls like --sort and --columns stay on `list`, because next
and search have opinions about order that are the reason they exist.

The reasoning for every remaining command is written down in the manual.

** import no longer drops dependencies

`cairn import` silently discarded every dependency in the document it was
reading.  It matched each written item back to the record that produced it by
comparing `source`, which a cairn export does not carry — the field was
synthesised locally for the item and left absent on the record, so the two never
compared equal.  Nothing failed and `cairn check` was satisfied; the backlog was
simply missing the thing `cairn next` uses to decide what is startable.

Found by a new property test rather than by reading.

** Testing where the defects have been

The soak test now covers every mutating command, `renumber --compact`, notes,
milestones, the read-only views, and the MCP server, and asserts at the end that
each operation actually ran — an operation the table stops reaching tests
nothing.  A second soak races four processes writing at once.

`tests/fuzz_args.rs` drives the real binary with arbitrary argument vectors and
asserts that cairn may refuse anything but may not panic.  32,000 invocations
found nothing, which is worth stating plainly.

`tests/interchange.rs` states the export/import round trip as a property, and
`tests/filter_laws.rs` states the filter grammar's laws: negation partitions the
backlog, alternation is union, a comma is intersection and does not care about
order.

** Changing several items at once

`cairn set` takes a list of ids, matching close, reopen, release and remove,
which all did already:

  cairn set 1 2 3 priority=p0
  cairn set --filter 'milestone=v0.1,status=backlog' priority=p1

A filtered write prints what it matched and asks before touching anything
unless --yes is given, in the same shape cairn remove uses; an unanswered
question is answered no.  Assignments are parsed before anything is written, so
a typo in the last one does not leave the first applied to half the items, and
the whole operation takes one lock.

** An item's history

`cairn log 12` shows how an item reached its present state, read from the
repository it lives in and reported as field transitions rather than as a
patch.  `--patch` hands the diffs to git, `--json` is for callers, and `-n`
limits the output.  Outside a git repository, or before an item is committed,
it explains rather than fails.

Renames are followed, so retitling an item does not lose its history.  Two
things about that needed care.  `git log --follow --reverse` silently truncates
the history, so the log is read newest-first and reversed afterwards.  And
because every cairn item has the same shape, git's rename detection concludes
that item 2 was renamed from item 1 and follows into the wrong item's history;
each revision is checked against the id it carries and the trail cut where that
changes.

In a shallow clone the oldest revision present is reported as such rather than
as the creation, which is a claim the clone cannot support.

** Where to report a bug

`cairn --help` now ends with the address, in the shape the GNU Coding
Standards ask for: a program should tell you where to take a problem, because
the person having one has the program in front of them and nothing else.

`cairn --bug-report` prints what a maintainer asks for anyway — version,
platform, format number, schema shape, item count, hook count — and reports
nothing a reporter would mind publishing.  It runs without a project, and
describes a cairn.toml that will not parse rather than failing on it, which is
exactly when somebody reaches for it.

** Choosing an editor

`cairn edit` falls back to notepad on Windows, where there is no vi, rather
than failing with a message about a missing program.  A variable that is set
but empty is no longer treated as a choice of editor: an empty VISUAL falls
through to EDITOR instead of shadowing it.  When the chosen editor is not on
PATH, cairn says which variable to set.

** Rules that check themselves

CONTRIBUTING.md now carries three rules, each enforced by tests/rules.rs: a new
command must make a real task harder to do without it, a change to what is
written to disk edits spec/README.md first, and there is never a library
target.

`cairn migrate` is hidden from --help.  It exists so that the migration path is
exercised before anybody needs it, which is a good reason to have the command
and a poor reason to spend a line of help output on it; it remains documented
in the manual, and the test says to unhide it as soon as there is genuinely a
format to migrate from.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0-rc.1 (2026-09-05)

** Storage

Items are Markdown files with YAML frontmatter, under a schema declared in
cairn.toml.  The format is specified normatively in the manual and is fixed at
format 1; see "Compatibility" for what may change and when.

Writes are atomic: every file is written beside its target, flushed, and
renamed over it, so an interruption leaves either the old contents or the new
ones.  Line endings are preserved, so a checkout configured with core.autocrlf
does not rewrite the whole backlog.

Mutating commands take a lock so that concurrent writers cannot allocate the
same identifier.  Reads never take it.

** Commands

  cairn init, new, list, show, set, close, reopen, edit, remove
  cairn next, claim, release, search    — finding and taking work
  cairn board, roadmap, render          — views, and the generated ROADMAP.md
  cairn check, renumber, migrate        — validation and repair
  cairn milestone, config               — the schema
  cairn import, export                  — the interchange format
  cairn agent, mcp                      — instructions and tools for agents
  cairn completions, man                — shell completions and manual pages

** Agents

`cairn mcp` serves the backlog over the Model Context Protocol, exposing ten
tools.  `cairn agent` writes an instruction block generated from the project's
own schema.  Items carry their dependency state, so an agent can tell what is
ready to start.

** Platforms

Linux is tier 1; macOS and Windows are tier 2.  The full test suite runs on all
three.  See "Platforms" in the manual.


